    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Somalia's president accuses UAE of 'interference' in his country",
    summary: "Somalia's president accuses UAE of 'interference' in his country The president of Somalia has accused the United Arab Emirates of \"interference\" in his country's internal affairs. Speaking in a televised address, Hassan Sheikh Mohamud said the UAE had established a military presence, including bases, outside Mogadishu’s national security framework without the government's approval. He said the country had also funded armed groups and opponents of the central government. \"Somalia is not a thoroughfare to be traversed illegally - it is not a commodity to be bought, nor is it a place that one can enter or leave at will,\" Hassan Sheikh said. Tensions between the UAE and Somalia have been high for many years, in part due to the former's support for the breakaway nation of Somaliland. country",
    preview: "Somalia's president accuses UAE of 'interference' in his country The president of Somalia has accused the United Arab Emirates of \"interference\" in his country's internal affairs. Speaking in a televised address, Hassan Sheikh Mohamud said the UAE had established a military presence, including bases, outside Mogadishu’s national security framework without the government's approval. He said the country had also funded armed groups and opponents of the central government.",
    body: "Somalia's president accuses UAE of 'interference' in his country The president of Somalia has accused the United Arab Emirates of \"interference\" in his country's internal affairs. Speaking in a televised address, Hassan Sheikh Mohamud said the UAE had established a military presence, including bases, outside Mogadishu’s national security framework without the government's approval. He said the country had also funded armed groups and opponents of the central government.\n\n\"Somalia is not a thoroughfare to be traversed illegally - it is not a commodity to be bought, nor is it a place that one can enter or leave at will,\" Hassan Sheikh said. Tensions between the UAE and Somalia have been high for many years, in part due to the former's support for the breakaway nation of Somaliland. country",
