    body: "Somalia's president accuses UAE of 'interference' in the country Submitted by MEE staff on Wed, 11/18/2026 - 10:19 Hassan Sheikh Mohamud says Gulf state has funded armed groups and established bases in breakaway Somaliland President of Somalia Hassan Sheikh Mohamud addresses the 81st session of the United Nations (UN) General Assembly in New York City on 24 September (AFP/Alexi J. Rosenfeld) Off The president of Somalia has accused the UAE of \"interference\" in his country's internal affairs.\n\nSpeaking in a televised address, Hassan Sheikh Mohamud said the UAE had established a military presence, including bases, outside Mogadishu’s national security framework without the government's approval. He said the country had also funded armed groups and opponents of the central government. \"Somalia is not a thoroughfare to be traversed illegally - it is not a commodity to be bought, nor is it a place that one can enter or leave at will,\" Hassan Sheikh said.\n\nTensions between the UAE and Somalia have been high for many years, in part due to the former's support for the breakaway nation of Somaliland. Mogadishu annulled all agreements with the UAE earlier this year and closed its airspace to Emirati military and cargo aircraft. Israel's first ambassador to Somaliland has credentials accepted ts of Berbera, Bosaso and Kismayo without government approval.\n\nIt also says the UAE played a role in Israel becoming the first country in the world to recognise Somaliland. Israel recognised Somaliland in January and is eyeing a military base in the port of Berbera alongside the UAE. DP World, the Emirati logistics company, runs the main port there, as part of a partnership with the British government’s foreign investment arm. Somaliland declared its independence in 1991 after a decade of war, escaping the civil war and instability that later engulfed Somalia.\n\nDiplomacy News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
