    id: "0e54c30287ec4bccddc2",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Axes, cutlass and blunt objects: Nigeria's campaign trail of violence",
    summary: "As Nigeria's January 2027 polls inch closer, a spate of violence and political thuggery during campaign rallies and town hall meetings is raising concerns about the safety of citizens participating in the election.",
    preview: "November 18, 2026 As Nigeria's January 2027 polls inch closer, a spate of violence and political thuggery during campaign rallies and town hall meetings is raising concerns about the safety of citizens participating in the election. Samuel Adebanjo had gone to the Oregun area of Lagos, the economic heartbeat of Nigeria, to cover a town hall meeting organized by the opposition party, the National Democratic Congress (NDC).",
    body: "November 18, 2026\n\nAs Nigeria's January 2027 polls inch closer, a spate of violence and political thuggery during campaign rallies and town hall meetings is raising concerns about the safety of citizens participating in the election.\n\nSamuel Adebanjo had gone to the Oregun area of Lagos, the economic heartbeat of Nigeria, to cover a town hall meeting organized by the opposition party, the National Democratic Congress (NDC). But what was supposed to be a routine reporting assignment took a violent turn when a group of armed men stormed the venue and disrupted the gathering. \"I was at the entrance, taking photos of people entering the hall when some buses drove in and I heard a loud bang,\" Samuel recalled. Before he could process what was happening, the men got down and began chasing supporters of the party with axes, cutlasses, and wooden planks, forcing many to flee for safety.\n\nAdebanjo ran towards the parking lot, hoping to find somewhere to hide. But some of the attackers spotted him and gave chase. The journalist with the local newspaper, The Cable, was surrounded by five men who started hitting him to make him let go of his camera. Despite wearing a press jacket, they accused him of being the party's cameraman. \"They wanted to collect the camera. I was struggling with them because I didn't want them to take it away. In the process, my middle finger was broken,\" he said.\n\nDuring the struggle, one of the thugs raised his axe and struck it towards his wrist. \"I used my hand to guard it. I was expecting blood. I never knew it landed on my wristwatch, and that's when it hit me that they're not joking, and that I had to let go of my equipment.\"\n\nHe was left groaning in agonizing pain. By the time the violence subsided and he was taken to the hospital, he had sustained a broken middle finger and internal injuries around his ribs. The attack also left several other people injured and properties, including vehicles belonging to supporters of the party, destroyed.\n\nAs the January 16, 2027 general election inches closer, incidents like this are becoming increasingly common, as incumbent Bola Tinubu battles for re-election on a ticket that revolves around combating insecurity and resolving the country's biting economic crisis.\n\nElection-related violence on the rise\n\nAdebanjo's experience is not an isolated incident. According to the National Peace Committee, 109 breaches of the National Peace Accord have been recorded across the country's 26 states and the federal capital, Abuja. Between August 19, when the campaign kicked off in the West African country, and October 27, political violence and thuggery, as well as misinformation and disinformation, top the violations, accounting for 33 and 29 incidents respectively.\n\nCampaign rallies, town hall meetings and other political events organized by parties have been marred by violence, threats and intimidation, prompting analysts to caution that rising violence could taint the credibility of the elections and discourage voter participation. On November 15, a group of men attacked the campaign convoy of former Kano state governor and National Democratic Congress vice-presidential candidate Rabiu Kwankwanso during a visit to the Bichi area of Kano. Similar incidents were recorded in both Sokoto and Kebbi when former Vice President Atiku Abubakar took his campaign to the northwestern states.\n\nCivil society groups, including Amnesty International, have condemned the spate of election-related violence and intimidation across the country, urging security agencies to bring perpetrators to book. President Tinubu has also condemned the rising incidents of violence at political events, warning that intimidation and political thuggery capable of disrupting public order would not be tolerated.\n\nNigeria's history of electoral violence\n\nPolitical violence is not new to Nigeria; nearly all of the country's elections have been tainted by violence and intimidation, including the deadly post-election violence that followed the 2011 elections, where at least 800 people were killed over days of rioting in states across northern Nigeria. Raheem Mutiu, a Nigerian researcher at Armed Conflict Location & Event Data (ACLED), a global organization that collects data on conflicts and political violence worldwide, told DW that the pattern ahead of the coming polls is shaping up to show \"political groups more agitated and determined to win elections by all means.\"\n\nMutiu described the spate of violence since the campaign was flagged off as mind-boggling. \"Within the period of two months, we have recorded over 30 politically linked attacks with 10 fatalities. But what this one points to is that politically linked attacks have become more frequent and geographically widespread ahead of the January 2027 general election,\" he said.\n\nThe country's multiparty politics has entrenched a winner-takes-all system that fuels fierce competition among parties and, at times, a willingness to resort to violence. There's also a growing intolerance for opposition among party supporters, although perpetrators are difficult to identify because they often act anonymously and as proxies for the interests they support.\n\n\"What we have seen again is that supporters across the political spectrum are involved in the attacks, but intensity of their violence and engagement largely depends on their local stronghold and territorial dominance,\" Mutiu added.\n\nThe high cost of violence\n\nA voter choosing under threat is not choosing freely, Dengiyefa Angalapu, a researcher at the Abuja-based Center for Democracy and Development, told DW. He noted that apart from the physical attacks, the spread of fake news and hate speech narratives, which form part of the non-physical breaches of the peace accord, \"can deceive voters and undermine trust in genuine information.\"\n\nAngalapu stressed that the growing threat of electoral violence could further undermine voter participation in a country already struggling with low turnout. More than 103 million Nigerians are registered to vote in the 2027 general elections, according to the country's electoral authority. However, the country has consistently recorded low voter participation, with the 2023 general elections witnessing the lowest turnout since Nigeria's return to democratic rule in 1999. Only 27% of the nearly 94 million registered voters cast their ballots.\n\n\"Intimidation adds another pressure: someone who fears punishment for their political preference may avoid meetings or withdraw from politics altogether,\" Angalapu added.\n\nAdebanjo is still recovering from his injuries. The attack has left him with a bigger question about safety ahead of the polls: \"If this could happen to me, a journalist, how much more the citizens? I am not sure, at this stage, people will even come out to vote.\"",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Deutsche Welle",
    url: "https://www.dw.com/en/axes-cutlass-and-blunt-objects-nigeria-s-campaign-trail-of-violence/a-79978467?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf",
